course: religion-ethics
lesson: 49
title: Rights, duties and community dilemmas
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Living together means rights and duties: you have a right to sleep, your neighbour has a right to enjoy music, and when rights clash they must be balanced, for example music off after ten at night.
explain: Free speech is precious, but so is not causing needless hurt: we can disagree with someone's religion without mocking what they hold holy.
explain: Children have a right to be heard on things that affect them, and groups often decide by voting, but the majority must still protect the minority.
explain: Jesus taught love of neighbour and welcome to the stranger, and many faiths teach the same; justice and mercy can work together, as when a wrongdoer puts right the harm they did.
explain: For a child today this means listening to both sides, speaking kindly about other people's faith, and helping to mend a broken friendship rather than only punishing.
fact: When two people's rights clash, such as a right to sleep and a right to play music, they need to be balanced.
fact: Free speech is precious, but so is not causing needless hurt to others.
fact: Disagreeing with someone's religion is fine; mocking what they hold holy causes needless hurt.
fact: The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child, article 12, says children have a right to be heard on things that affect them.
fact: In a democracy decisions are often made by voting, and the side with more votes, the majority, wins.
fact: Majority rule must still protect the rights of the minority; the majority is not always right.
fact: We have special duties to our own family and general duties to everyone, and both matter.
fact: Refugees are people forced to flee their homes because of war or persecution (being hunted or hurt for who you are or what you believe).
fact: Many faiths teach welcoming the stranger; how countries should respond to refugees is a disputed question.
fact: Punishment can protect people, show that a wrong was serious and help the wrongdoer change.
fact: Christians believe in forgiveness, and that justice and mercy can work together.
fact: Restorative justice brings the wrongdoer and the person harmed together to put things right.
exercise: A neighbour plays loud music late at night. How would you balance the rights? | Agree times: music is fine in the day, quiet late at night | The neighbour must never play music again | The neighbour can play music as loud as they like, at any hour, always | Play your own music louder to get back at them
exercise: Whose rights clash in the case of the noisy neighbour? | Your right to sleep and the neighbour's right to enjoy music | The neighbour's right to vote and your right to speak | Your right to keep a pet and the neighbour's right to a garden | Nobody's, since rights can never clash
exercise: Where is the line between free speech and cruelty? | You may disagree and argue, but not insult people to hurt them | There is no line at all: you can say anything you like to anyone | There is no free speech: you must never disagree with anyone at all | The line is only whatever a teacher happens to be able to hear
exercise: Why is it unkind to mock a classmate's faith? | It hurts them about what they hold holy, and adds nothing to a real disagreement | It is unkind only if their religion is the same as yours | It is fine, because religion is only a matter of opinion | It is unkind only if you do it on a Sunday
exercise: Which is a respectful way to disagree with a friend's religion? | Say what you believe and why, and listen to what they believe | Laugh at their prayers in front of the class | Tell them their whole family is stupid for believing such a thing | Refuse to sit next to them ever again
exercise: Which decisions should children help make? | Things that affect them, like playground rules or class trips | Nothing at all, because children never have any good ideas of their own | Everything, including the head teacher's pay and the school budget | Only the colour of the classroom walls and nothing more than that
exercise: What does article 12 of the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child say? | Children have a right to be heard on things that affect them | Children have a right to stay up as late as they like on school nights | Children have a right to vote in national elections | Children have a right to choose their own teachers
exercise: Is the majority always right? | No; the majority can be wrong and must protect the minority | Yes, the side with more votes is always right, whatever it decides | Yes, but only when the vote is held at school with the teacher watching | No, and so voting should never be used to decide anything at all
exercise: The class votes 20 to 5 to play football at every break, but 5 children cannot play because of injuries. What is the best response? | Find a way to include the 5, like other games on some days | The 20 win, so the 5 must just sit and watch every single day | The 5 should be moved to another school | Cancel break time for everyone
exercise: Is it fair to give more to your own family than to strangers? | Partly: we have special duties to family, but also duties to all in need | Yes, strangers deserve nothing at all from us | No, family should never get anything special | Yes, but only if the strangers are rich
exercise: Who are refugees? | People forced to flee war or persecution | People who go on holiday to another country | People who move house for a bigger garden | People who refuse to follow the rules
exercise: What could your school do to welcome a refugee child? | Give them a buddy, help with English and include them in games | Ask them to stay quiet until they speak perfect English | Leave them alone for a few weeks so they can sort themselves out | Tell them they should go back home soon
exercise: Which statement about refugees is fair? | Many faiths teach welcome; how countries should respond is disputed | Everyone agrees on exactly what every country should do | Refugees chose to leave just for fun | No religion has ever said anything at all about welcoming strangers
exercise: What is the point of punishment? | To protect people, show the wrong was serious and help the wrongdoer change | To make the punisher feel strong and important | To get revenge and make the wrongdoer suffer as much as possible | There is no point; wrongdoers should be ignored
exercise: Can punishment and forgiveness go together? | Yes, justice and mercy can work together | No, if you forgive you must never set any consequence | No, forgiveness is only for people who never did wrong | Yes, but only after the wrongdoer has been punished for years
exercise: What is restorative justice? | Bringing wrongdoer and victim together to put things right | Locking the wrongdoer away for ever and forgetting all about them | Letting the victim punish the wrongdoer however they like | Pretending the wrong never happened at all
exercise: How could a broken friendship be repaired after a fight? | Each listens to the other, says sorry and agrees how to put it right | Each tells everyone else how bad the other one is | The one who lost the fight must say sorry, and nobody else ever has to | Never speak again, as friendships can't be mended
test: Brother: "Free speech means I can say whatever I want about anyone, deal with it." What is the best reply? | Free speech matters, but so does not hurting people for no reason | You're right, words can never hurt anyone at all, only sticks and stones | Shut up, nobody cares what you say | Free speech means you must always agree with me
test: A Muslim classmate asks, "Why do some kids laugh when I pray?" What is the best thing to say? | That's unkind; we can believe different things and still respect each other | They laugh because your religion is funny | Just stop praying at school, then they'll stop | Ignore it, laughing is a kind of free speech
test: "Neighbour's music is SO loud at 2am, I'm gonna smash his speakers." Which response reasons best? | Talk to him calmly or ask an adult to help agree quiet hours | Smashing them is fair, since he started it and he deserves it | Do nothing about it at all and just never get a good night's sleep again | Play even louder music back at him all night to teach him a lesson
test: "The vote was 15 to 10, so the 10 losers just have to shut up." What is weak about this? | It ignores that the minority still has rights and deserves a hearing | It is too fair to the people who lost | It uses numbers, which have no place in ethics | Nothing; the winners can always do whatever they like with the losers
test: A judge asks: "Should you help your own brother first or a stranger in greater need?" Which answer is best? | Both duties matter; it depends how great the need is, so I'd weigh them | Always my brother first, because strangers don't matter to me at all, ever | Always the stranger, because family duties don't count | Neither, because helping is not my job
test: Your friend says, "Refugees are just people who want free stuff." How do you put it right? | Refugees are people forced to flee war or persecution | You're right, they all come here just to get free stuff | Refugees are just people going away on very long holidays | Refugees are people who break the law for fun wherever they go
test: Sister: "If I forgive Tom for breaking my bike, he gets away with it." What is a good answer? | You can forgive him and still ask him to help fix or pay for it | Forgiving means he must never have to fix or pay for anything at all | Don't forgive him, just break his bike back | Forgiving only works if he is your best friend
test: At school, Jake and Leo fought. The head teacher brings them together to say what happened and agree how to make up. What is this called? | Restorative justice | Free speech | Majority rule | A lottery
test: "Kids don't get a say in school rules, cos adults know best, end of." What is weak about this? | It gives no reason why children's views about their own school don't count | It respects children's voices too much | It is too long and full of reasons | Nothing; adults are never wrong
test: A boy from Ukraine joins your class and speaks little English. Which plan is kindest and wisest? | Be his buddy, show him around and include him in games | Wait for him to learn English before talking to him | Tell him to sit at the back so he isn't in anybody's way | Make jokes about his accent so he fits in
test: Which statement about free speech is true? | It is precious, but it does not make cruel words harmless | It means no one may ever disagree with anyone about anything | It means insults are always good for people and make them tougher | It only applies to grown-up people who are over the age of eighteen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relation between freedom of expression and respect for religious belief? | Disagreement is permissible, whereas mockery of that which others hold sacred occasions needless hurt | Freedom of expression authorises any utterance whatsoever, irrespective of its consequences | The holding of religious belief prohibits every expression of disagreement | The two are wholly unrelated, neither bearing upon the other
exam: Pursuant to article 12 of the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child, identify the entitlement thereby conferred. | The right of the child to be heard in matters affecting the child | The right of the child to vote in national elections | The right of the child to select the child's own teachers | The right of the child to determine the child's own hours of rest
exam: It being given that a decision has been reached by majority vote, select the response most consistent with sound democratic principle. | The rights of the minority remain to be protected, the majority not being infallible | The minority forfeits all entitlements upon the declaration of the result | The decision is invalid unless every participant has concurred | The majority is thereby shown to be correct in every particular
exam: Determine which of the following most accurately describes the practice termed restorative justice. | The bringing together of offender and injured party in order that the harm be repaired | The indefinite confinement of the offender without further consideration | The infliction upon the offender of whatever penalty the injured party may happen to desire | The concealment of the offence so that it may be forgotten
